# PHASE 7: dd TIMINGS FOR ALL SEVEN `fill` SWITCH STRUCTURES.
#
# WHY THIS WAS OPEN SINCE PLAN 2. The route census found 167 of 221 bootstrap instances that the
# released `auto` policy sends DENSE and the opt-in `fill` policy would send SPARSE. Those 167 fall
# into SEVEN distinct shapes (seven distinct m, all nBlock=17). dd timings existed for two of them,
# so `fill` stayed opt-in: "not the default because the timings behind that decision cover two
# problem structures, not seven."
#
# THIS FILE COVERS ALL SEVEN, so that sentence no longer holds.
#
# HOW THE INPUTS WERE OBTAINED. They are the census's OWN files, on Expanse under
# /expanse/lustre/scratch/zzheng8/temp_project/lorentzian/. One representative instance per
# structure was copied Expanse -> this workstation -> pi, with sha256 verified at every hop, and
# each file's m and nBlock re-read from the file itself rather than assumed:
#
#   m2439    smoke_new_min                                                     e454b96e2a8ec977...
#   m4489    dE_ladder/g1o100_b1_T4_L8L012_d1dL6_hkcheb/lower_min              93fa26794bc40bc9...
#   m5278    dE_ladder/g1o100_b1_T4_L8L012_d2dL6_hkcheb/lower_min              2a8eb4f2961a2fcf...
#   m6067    Tscan/g1o100_b1_T10_L8L012_d3dL6_hkcheb/lower_min                 01ae474395943bb8...
#   m8359    Tscan_dL8/g1o100_b1_T11o2_L8L012_d3dL8_hkcheb/lower_min           a729e6355ee4deb6...
#   m10614   G1_dL6_rest/g1_b1_T11_L8L012_d6_hkcheb/upper_min                  9beab98adb520903...
#   m11227   Tscan_dL10/g1o100_b1_T10_L8L012_d3dL10_hkcheb/lower_min           7be7e44b05436b13...
#
# COMPUTE RAN ON pi, NOT ON EXPANSE. The standing instruction is not to disturb the existing
# sdpa_gmp installation there; copying 52 MB of input out is the whole of the interaction with it.
#
# HOST: pi -- i9-13900K, 24 physical cores, Ubuntu. OMP_PROC_BIND=true OMP_PLACES=cores
#       OMP_MAX_ACTIVE_LEVELS=1, OMP_NUM_THREADS=24, taskset -c 0-23. 3 repeats, medians.
# BUILD: dev at the bitset commit (post-v7.1.3-omp.3).
# PROTOCOL: shipped param.sdpa with maxIteration -> 4 -- the same fixed-budget protocol as every
#       other timing in this campaign. These are PER-ITERATION costs, not times to solution; see
#       the limitation below.
#
# ---- RESULT: `fill` WINS ON ALL SEVEN, ON BOTH TIME AND MEMORY ----
#
#     m    instances |  auto (DENSE)      |  fill (SPARSE)     | faster | less memory
#   2439           1 |    1.924 s  116 MB |   0.606 s   50 MB  |  3.17x |  2.30x
#   4489           2 |    9.219 s  392 MB |   3.593 s  213 MB  |  2.57x |  1.84x
#   5278           2 |   14.502 s  519 MB |   3.877 s  234 MB  |  3.74x |  2.22x
#   6067          52 |   22.974 s  671 MB |   4.498 s  277 MB  |  5.11x |  2.42x
#   8359          24 |   66.203 s 1289 MB |  15.131 s  587 MB  |  4.38x |  2.20x
#  10614          38 |  150.473 s 2032 MB |  22.362 s  831 MB  |  6.73x |  2.45x
#  11227          48 |  175.807 s 2358 MB |  47.944 s 1183 MB  |  3.67x |  1.99x
#
# One representative from EACH of the seven structural classes was measured, and those seven classes
# contain all 167 switch instances. That is complete coverage of the CLASSES -- seven timed inputs,
# not 167. Equal dimensions and block structure constrain the work but do not prove identical
# trajectories or timings for every member of a class. The advantage is 2.6x-6.7x in time and 1.8x-2.5x in memory, and it
# does not shrink with problem size -- the largest structure still gains 3.7x on half the memory,
# and at m=10614 the dense route needs 2.0 GB where fill needs 0.8 GB.
#
# ---- WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE ----
#
# It settles the objection that kept `fill` opt-in: the timings now cover all seven structures, not
# two, and every one favours `fill`.
#
# It does NOT by itself justify flipping the default, for a reason this project has been careful
# about throughout: SDPA_BMAT_MODE is RESULT-CHANGING. Dense and sparse are different factorisation
# routes with different iterate trajectories -- unlike the assembly's serial-vs-threaded choice,
# which is byte-identical. A 4-iteration budget measures per-iteration cost; it cannot show whether
# the two routes need the same NUMBER of iterations to reach a given tolerance. These instances do
# not converge at double-double precision under the tolerances this campaign has tested, so a
# time-to-solution comparison does not exist to be made.
#
# So the honest statement is: on per-iteration cost and peak memory, `fill` dominates `auto` on
# every structure that distinguishes them, by wide margins. Whether that is enough to change a
# result-changing default is a judgement for the owner, and it is now a judgement with complete
# evidence under it rather than two data points.
